-> Learn Java-based Spring configuration using @Configuration and @Bean.

    What is Java-Based Configuration?
    Instead of annotations on classes, we define beans manually in Java using:
    @Configuration
    @Bean
  Replaces XML completely.

-> Annotation vs Java-Based Configuration

    | Annotation-Based | Java-Based           |
    | ---------------- | -------------------- |
    | @Component       | @Bean                |
    | Auto scanning    | Manual bean creation |
    | Less control     | Full control         |
    | Faster           | More explicit        |

-> @Configuration
       Marks a class as Spring configuration class.
      @Configuration
      public class AppConfig {
      }

-> @Bean – Define Bean
    @Bean
    public Employee employee() {
        return new Employee();
    }

-> Inject Dependencies Between @Bean Methods
    @Bean
    public EmployeeService employeeService() {
        return new EmployeeService(employee());
    }
Spring ensures same singleton instance is used.




-> Java-Based Configuration Example

    public class Employee {
        public void show() {
            System.out.println("Employee Bean");
        }
    }


    public class EmployeeService {

        private Employee employee;

        public EmployeeService(Employee employee) {
            this.employee = employee;
        }

        public void display() {
            employee.show();
            System.out.println("Java Config Service");
        }
    }

    @Configuration
    public class AppConfig {

        @Bean
        public Employee employee() {
            return new Employee();
        }

        @Bean
        public EmployeeService employeeService() {
            return new EmployeeService(employee());
        }
    }

    public class MainApp {

        public static void main(String[] args) {
            ApplicationContext context =
                    new AnnotationConfigApplicationContext(AppConfig.class);

            EmployeeService service = context.getBean(EmployeeService.class);
            service.display();
        }
    }

    public class MainApp {

        public static void main(String[] args) {
            ApplicationContext context =
                    new AnnotationConfigApplicationContext(AppConfig.class);

            EmployeeService service = context.getBean(EmployeeService.class);
            service.display();
        }
    }

-> When to Prefer Java-Based Configuration?

       ✔ When you need:
       Full control over beans
       Third-party class beans
       Conditional logic
       Clear configuration separation

